Looking at the code fragments, I can see that Hexi provides a `binary_stream` class for reading from buffers, and it supports operations like reading values with the `>>` operator and skipping data with the `skip()` method.

Based on the examples, here's the complete implementation:

```cpp
#include <hexi/hexi.h>
#include <cstdint>
#include <span>

struct MessageHeader {
    uint16_t message_type;
    uint32_t body_length;
    uint32_t footer;
};

MessageHeader read_header_and_skip_body(const std::span<const uint8_t> buffer) {
    // Create a buffer adaptor from the span
    hexi::buffer_adaptor adaptor(buffer);

    // Create a binary stream from the adaptor
    hexi::binary_stream stream(adaptor);

    // Create the result structure
    MessageHeader header;

    // Read message_type (uint16_t)
    stream >> header.message_type;

    // Read body_length (uint32_t)
    stream >> header.body_length;

    // Skip the body_data using the length we just read
    stream.skip(header.body_length);

    // Read footer (uint32_t)
    stream >> header.footer;

    return header;
}
```

This implementation:
1. Creates a `hexi::buffer_adaptor` from the input `std::span<const uint8_t>` buffer
2. Creates a `hexi::binary_stream` from the adaptor to read binary data
3. Uses the `>>` operator to read `message_type` (uint16_t) 
4. Uses the `>>` operator to read `body_length` (uint32_t)
5. Uses `skip()` to skip over the body data based on the length we just read
6. Uses the `>>` operator to read `footer` (uint32_t)
7. Returns the populated `MessageHeader` struct

