Looking at the code fragments and the Hexi library usage patterns, I can see that:

1. For `read_packet_header`: I need to use `span()` to get a zero-copy view of the header data
2. For `read_message_until_delimiter`: I need to use `view()` to get a zero-copy string view up to the null terminator

Based on the test files, I can see that:
- `span()` returns a `std::span` of raw bytes for a specific size
- `view()` reads a null-terminated string and returns a `std::string_view`

Here's the complete implementation:

```cpp
#include <hexi/hexi.h>
#include <cstdint>
#include <span>
#include <string_view>

struct PacketHeader {
    uint8_t version;
    uint8_t flags;
    uint16_t packet_id;
};

PacketHeader read_packet_header(const std::span<const uint8_t> buffer) {
    // TODO: use span() to read header with zero-copy
    hexi::buffer_adaptor adaptor(buffer);
    hexi::binary_stream stream(adaptor);

    // Get a zero-copy span of the header data
    auto header_span = stream.span(sizeof(PacketHeader));

    // Create PacketHeader from the span
    PacketHeader header;
    header.version = header_span[0];
    header.flags = header_span[1];
    // packet_id is uint16_t, so we need to read 2 bytes
    header.packet_id = static_cast<uint16_t>(header_span[2]) | 
                       (static_cast<uint16_t>(header_span[3]) << 8);

    return header;
}

std::string_view read_message_until_delimiter(const std::span<const uint8_t> buffer) {
    // TODO: use view() to read null-terminated string with zero-copy
    // Create a span of const char from the uint8_t buffer
    std::span<const char> char_buffer(
        reinterpret_cast<const char*>(buffer.data()), 
        buffer.size()
    );

    hexi::buffer_adaptor adaptor(char_buffer);
    hexi::binary_stream stream(adaptor);

    // Use view() to get a zero-copy string_view of the null-terminated string
    return stream.view();
}
```

