output.text:
Looking at the code fragments, I can see that the Hexi library uses `binary_stream` for reading/writing binary data, and supports reading containers. Based on the test examples showing how to read values using the `>>` operator and how containers work, here's the complete implementation:

```cpp
#include <hexi/hexi.h>
#include <vector>
#include <cstdint>
#include <span>

std::vector<uint32_t> read_item_batch(const std::span<const uint8_t> buffer) {
    // Create a buffer adaptor from the input span
    hexi::buffer_adaptor adaptor(buffer);

    // Create a binary stream from the adaptor
    hexi::binary_stream stream(adaptor);

    // Read the item count (uint32_t)
    std::uint32_t item_count = 0;
    stream >> item_count;

    // Create a vector to hold the items
    std::vector<std::uint32_t> items(item_count);

    // Read all items from the stream
    for (std::uint32_t i = 0; i < item_count; ++i) {
        stream >> items[i];
    }

    return items;
}
```

This implementation:
1. Creates a `hexi::buffer_adaptor` from the input buffer span to make it compatible with Hexi
2. Creates a `hexi::binary_stream` from the adaptor for convenient binary reading operations
3. Reads the `item_count` as a `uint32_t` using the stream extraction operator (`>>`)
4. Pre-allocates a vector with the appropriate size
5. Reads each `uint32_t` item from the stream using the extraction operator in a loop
6. Returns the populated vector

