Looking at the code fragments, I can see that `block_allocator` is a memory allocator that uses a free list internally. The `push()` method adds blocks to the free list (used during deallocation), and `pop()` removes blocks from the free list (used during allocation).

From the code fragments, I can see:
1. `deallocate()` calls `push()` to return blocks to the free list
2. `initialise_free_list()` calls `push()` to initialize all blocks as free
3. The allocator tracks various statistics with debug macros

Here's the complete implementation:

```cpp
#include <hexi/allocators/block_allocator.h>
#include <cstdint>
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

// Note: push() and pop() are private, but we can observe their behavior
// through allocate() and deallocate() operations
template<size_t element_count = 10>
void demonstrate_block_allocator_behavior() {
    // Create a block_allocator for uint64_t with specified element count
    // This internally initializes the free list by calling push() for each block
    hexi::block_allocator<std::uint64_t, element_count> allocator;

    std::cout << "=== Block Allocator Free List Management Demo ===" << std::endl;
    std::cout << "Allocator capacity: " << element_count << " blocks" << std::endl;

#ifdef HEXI_DEBUG_ALLOCATORS
    std::cout << "\nInitial state (all blocks in free list):" << std::endl;
    std::cout << "  Total allocations: " << allocator.total_allocs << std::endl;
    std::cout << "  Active allocations: " << allocator.active_count << std::endl;
#endif

    // Store pointers to demonstrate free list behavior
    std::vector<std::uint64_t*> allocations;

    // Allocate blocks - each allocate() internally calls pop() to remove from free list
    std::cout << "\n--- Allocating blocks (pop from free list) ---" << std::endl;
    for (size_t i = 0; i < element_count / 2; ++i) {
        auto* block = allocator.allocate();
        *block = i * 100;  // Write some data
        allocations.push_back(block);
        std::cout << "Allocated block " << i << ", value: " << *block << std::endl;
    }

#ifdef HEXI_DEBUG_ALLOCATORS
    std::cout << "\nAfter allocations:" << std::endl;
    std::cout << "  Total allocations: " << allocator.total_allocs << std::endl;
    std::cout << "  Active allocations: " << allocator.active_count << std::endl;
    std::cout << "  Storage active: " << allocator.storage_active_count << std::endl;
#endif

    // Deallocate some blocks - each deallocate() internally calls push() to add to free list
    std::cout << "\n--- Deallocating blocks (push to free list) ---" << std::endl;
    for (size_t i = 0; i < 2 && !allocations.empty(); ++i) {
        auto* block = allocations.back();
        std::cout << "Deallocating block with value: " << *block << std::endl;
        allocator.deallocate(block);
        allocations.pop_back();
    }

#ifdef HEXI_DEBUG_ALLOCATORS
    std::cout << "\nAfter some deallocations:" << std::endl;
    std::cout << "  Total allocations: " << allocator.total_allocs << std::endl;
    std::cout << "  Total deallocations: " << allocator.total_deallocs << std::endl;
    std::cout << "  Active allocations: " << allocator.active_count << std::endl;
#endif

    // Allocate again - will reuse blocks from free list (pop)
    std::cout << "\n--- Re-allocating (reusing freed blocks via pop) ---" << std::endl;
    for (size_t i = 0; i < 2; ++i) {
        auto* block = allocator.allocate();
        *block = 999 + i;
        allocations.push_back(block);
        std::cout << "Re-allocated block " << i << ", new value: " << *block << std::endl;
    }

#ifdef HEXI_DEBUG_ALLOCATORS
    std::cout << "\nAfter re-allocations:" << std::endl;
    std::cout << "  Total allocations: " << allocator.total_allocs << std::endl;
    std::cout << "  Active allocations: " << allocator.active_count << std::endl;
#endif

    // Clean up remaining allocations
    std::cout << "\n--- Cleaning up (pushing all back to free list) ---" << std::endl;
    for (auto* block : allocations) {
        allocator.deallocate(block);
    }

#ifdef HEXI_DEBUG_ALLOCATORS
    std::cout << "\nFinal state:" << std::endl;
    std::cout << "  Total allocations: " << allocator.total_allocs << std::endl;
    std::cout << "  Total deallocations: " << allocator.total_deallocs << std::endl;
    std::cout << "  Active allocations: " << allocator.active_count << std::endl;
#endif

    std::cout << "\n=== Summary ===" << std::endl;
    std::cout << "The free list operates as a LIFO (stack) structure:" << std::endl;
    std::cout << "  - push() adds deallocated blocks to the head of the free list" << std::endl;
    std::cout << "  - pop() removes and returns the head block from the free list" << std::endl;
    std::cout << "  - Initialization pushes all blocks onto the free list" << std::endl;
    std::cout << "  - allocate() internally calls pop()" << std::endl;
    std::cout << "  - deallocate() internally calls push()" << std::endl;
}
```

This implementation demonstrates:
1. **Initialization**: The allocator initializes the free list by pushing all blocks
2. **Allocation (pop)**: When allocating, blocks are popped from the free list head
3. **Deallocation (push)**: When deallocating, blocks are pushed back to the free list head
4. **Reuse**: Deallocated blocks can be reused for new allocations (LIFO order)
5. **Statistics tracking**: When compiled with `HEXI_DEBUG_ALLOCATORS`, shows allocation counts

