Looking at the code fragments, I can see that `hexi::file_buffer` is used to read data from files. Based on the test examples, particularly `TEST(file_buffer, skip)` and `TEST(file_buffer, initial_size)`, I can see that:

1. `file_buffer` has a constructor that takes a `std::filesystem::path`
2. It has a `read()` method that takes a pointer to read data into
3. The buffer can read typed data directly

Here's the completed implementation:

```cpp
#include <hexi/file_buffer.h>
#include <cstdint>
#include <filesystem>

int32_t read_int32_from_file(const std::filesystem::path& file_path) {
    hexi::file_buffer buffer(file_path);
    int32_t value = 0;
    buffer.read(&value);
    return value;
}

float read_float_from_file(const std::filesystem::path& file_path) {
    hexi::file_buffer buffer(file_path);
    float value = 0.0f;
    buffer.read(&value);
    return value;
}
```

The implementation:
1. Creates a `hexi::file_buffer` object with the given file path
2. Declares a variable of the appropriate type (int32_t or float) initialized to 0
3. Uses the `read()` method to read the data from the file buffer into the variable
4. Returns the read value

The `read()` method automatically reads `sizeof(T)` bytes from the file based on the pointer type passed to it, making it straightforward to read typed data.

