Looking at the code fragments, I can see that the Hexi library provides `serialise()` and `deserialise()` methods on the binary stream classes. Based on the code fragment from `binary_stream_reader.h`, I can see that `deserialise()` creates a `stream_read_adaptor` and calls the object's `serialise()` method with it.

For serialization, based on the pattern in the examples (like `serialise_custom_type.cpp` and `polymorphic_binary_stream.cpp`), the writer likely has a similar `serialise()` method that creates a write adaptor.

Here's the complete implementation:

```cpp
#include <hexi/pmc/binary_stream_writer.h>
#include <hexi/pmc/binary_stream_reader.h>
#include <hexi/pmc/buffer_adaptor.h>
#include <vector>
#include <cstdint>

struct UserData {
    uint32_t user_id;
    std::string name;

    void serialise(auto& stream) {
        stream << user_id << name;
    }
};

void serialize_object(hexi::pmc::binary_stream_writer& writer, const UserData& data) {
    writer.serialise(data);
}

void deserialize_object(hexi::pmc::binary_stream_reader& reader, UserData& data) {
    reader.deserialise(data);
}
```

The implementation is straightforward:
- `serialize_object`: Calls the `serialise()` method on the writer, which internally creates a write adaptor and passes it to the object's `serialise()` method
- `deserialize_object`: Calls the `deserialise()` method on the reader, which internally creates a read adaptor and passes it to the object's `serialise()` method

The beauty of this design is that the same `serialise()` method in `UserData` works for both serialization and deserialization - the stream adaptor (read or write) handles the direction automatically through operator overloading.

