output.text:
Looking at the code fragments, I can see how the Hexi library works with binary streams. The key patterns I notice are:

1. For writing containers with a prefixed count, the library uses `hexi::prefixed()` which writes a `uint32_t` count followed by the elements
2. For reading, it reads the count first, then reads the elements
3. The stream operators `<<` and `>>` are used for writing and reading

Based on the test examples showing `stream << hexi::prefixed(input)` and `stream >> hexi::prefixed(output)`, and the implementation hints in `binary_stream_reader.h` showing the `prefixed<T>` adaptor reads a `uint32_t` count and then calls `read_container()`, here's the complete implementation:

```cpp
#include <hexi/pmc/binary_stream_writer.h>
#include <hexi/pmc/binary_stream_reader.h>
#include <hexi/pmc/buffer_adaptor.h>
#include <vector>
#include <cstdint>

void write_container_to_stream(hexi::pmc::binary_stream_writer& writer, const std::vector<uint32_t>& container) {
    // Write the count of elements as uint32_t
    uint32_t count = static_cast<uint32_t>(container.size());
    writer << count;

    // Write each element
    for (const auto& element : container) {
        writer << element;
    }
}

std::vector<uint32_t> read_container_from_stream(hexi::pmc::binary_stream_reader& reader) {
    // Read the count of elements
    uint32_t count = 0;
    reader >> count;

    // Read elements
    std::vector<uint32_t> container;
    container.reserve(count);

    for (uint32_t i = 0; i < count; ++i) {
        uint32_t element = 0;
        reader >> element;
        container.push_back(element);
    }

    return container;
}
```

This implementation:
1. **`write_container_to_stream`**: First writes the container size as a `uint32_t`, then iterates through and writes each element using the `<<` operator
2. **`read_container_from_stream`**: First reads the count as a `uint32_t`, then reads that many elements one by one using the `>>` operator, building up the returned vector

