Looking at the code fragments, I can see that `buffer_adaptor` has a `copy()` method that copies data without advancing the read pointer. From the fragments, I can see:

1. The non-template version: `void copy(void* destination, std::size_t length) const`
2. There should be a template version for copying single values (similar to how `read()` works)

Based on the patterns in the test files and the API usage, here's the complete implementation:

```cpp
#include <hexi/pmc/buffer_adaptor.h>
#include <vector>
#include <cstdint>
#include <cstring>

void peek_data_from_buffer(hexi::pmc::buffer_adaptor<std::vector<std::byte>>& buffer, void* data, std::size_t length) {
    // Copy data without advancing the read pointer using the non-template version of copy()
    buffer.copy(data, length);
}

template<typename T>
void peek_value_from_buffer(hexi::pmc::buffer_adaptor<std::vector<std::byte>>& buffer, T& value) {
    // Copy a single value without advancing the read pointer using the template version
    buffer.copy(&value, sizeof(T));
}

bool peek_and_compare(hexi::pmc::buffer_adaptor<std::vector<std::byte>>& buffer, const void* expected, std::size_t length) {
    // Allocate temporary storage for peeked data
    std::vector<std::byte> temp(length);

    // Peek data from buffer without consuming it
    buffer.copy(temp.data(), length);

    // Compare peeked data with expected data
    return std::memcmp(temp.data(), expected, length) == 0;
}
