Looking at the code fragments, I can see how the `hexi::pmc::buffer_adaptor` works. Let me implement the three functions:

```cpp
#include <hexi/pmc/buffer_adaptor.h>
#include <hexi/shared.h>
#include <vector>
#include <cstdint>

bool check_can_write_seek(hexi::pmc::buffer_adaptor<std::vector<std::byte>>& buffer) {
    // Check if the buffer supports write seeking
    return buffer.can_write_seek();
}

void seek_write_position(hexi::pmc::buffer_adaptor<std::vector<std::byte>>& buffer, hexi::buffer_seek direction, std::size_t offset) {
    // Seek the write position using write_seek() with different seek directions
    buffer.write_seek(direction, offset);
}

void update_header_at_position(hexi::pmc::buffer_adaptor<std::vector<std::byte>>& buffer, uint32_t header_value) {
    // Save current position, write header placeholder, write body, then seek back to update header

    // Write a placeholder header (4 bytes for uint32_t)
    uint32_t placeholder = 0;
    buffer.write(&placeholder, sizeof(uint32_t));

    // Save the position after the header (which is now at offset 4)
    std::size_t body_position = sizeof(uint32_t);

    // Write some body data (example)
    const char* body_data = "Body content";
    std::size_t body_length = 12; // length of "Body content"
    buffer.write(body_data, body_length);

    // Seek back to the beginning to update the header
    buffer.write_seek(hexi::buffer_seek::sk_absolute, 0);

    // Write the actual header value
    buffer.write(&header_value, sizeof(uint32_t));

    // Seek back to the end (after body) to continue writing if needed
    buffer.write_seek(hexi::buffer_seek::sk_absolute, body_position + body_length);
}
```

This implementation:

1. **`check_can_write_seek`**: Calls the `can_write_seek()` method on the buffer adaptor to check if write seeking is supported.

2. **`seek_write_position`**: Uses the `write_seek()` method with the provided direction and offset. The direction can be:
   - `hexi::buffer_seek::sk_absolute`: Seek to an absolute position
   - `hexi::buffer_seek::sk_forward`: Seek forward by offset bytes
   - `hexi::buffer_seek::sk_backward`: Seek backward by offset bytes

3. **`update_header_at_position`**: 
   - First writes a placeholder header (zeros) at the beginning
   - Writes body content after the header
   - Seeks back to the absolute position 0 (beginning)
   - Updates the header with the actual value
   - Seeks to the end to restore the write position

