Looking at the code fragments, I can see that the Hexi library has built-in support for varint encoding/decoding. From the fragments, I can see:

1. There's a `varint_decode` function used in `binary_stream_reader`
2. The library uses a standard varint encoding where each byte uses 7 bits for data and 1 bit as a continuation flag
3. The stream writer/reader classes handle the low-level I/O

Based on the test patterns and the library's structure, here's the complete implementation:

```cpp
#include <hexi/shared.h>
#include <hexi/pmc/buffer_adaptor.h>
#include <hexi/pmc/binary_stream_writer.h>
#include <hexi/pmc/binary_stream_reader.h>
#include <vector>
#include <cstdint>

using namespace hexi::detail;

template<typename T>
std::size_t encode_varint(hexi::pmc::binary_stream_writer& writer, T value) {
    static_assert(std::is_integral_v<T>, "T must be an integral type");

    std::size_t bytes_written = 0;

    // Convert signed to unsigned to handle bit operations correctly
    using UnsignedT = std::make_unsigned_t<T>;
    UnsignedT uvalue = static_cast<UnsignedT>(value);

    // Encode using varint format (7 bits per byte, MSB indicates continuation)
    do {
        std::uint8_t byte = uvalue & 0x7F;  // Take lower 7 bits
        uvalue >>= 7;                        // Shift right by 7 bits

        if (uvalue != 0) {
            byte |= 0x80;  // Set continuation bit if more bytes follow
        }

        writer.put(&byte, 1);
        bytes_written++;
    } while (uvalue != 0);

    return bytes_written;
}

template<typename T>
T decode_varint(hexi::pmc::binary_stream_reader& reader) {
    static_assert(std::is_integral_v<T>, "T must be an integral type");

    using UnsignedT = std::make_unsigned_t<T>;
    UnsignedT result = 0;
    int shift = 0;

    // Decode varint format
    while (true) {
        std::uint8_t byte = 0;
        reader.get(&byte, 1);

        // Add the lower 7 bits to result
        result |= static_cast<UnsignedT>(byte & 0x7F) << shift;

        // If continuation bit is not set, we're done
        if ((byte & 0x80) == 0) {
            break;
        }

        shift += 7;

        // Prevent overflow for small types
        if (shift >= sizeof(T) * 8) {
            break;
        }
    }

    return static_cast<T>(result);
}

template<typename T>
bool encode_decode_roundtrip(T original_value) {
    // Create a buffer and stream for encoding
    std::vector<char> buffer;
    hexi::pmc::buffer_adaptor adaptor(buffer);
    hexi::pmc::binary_stream_writer writer(adaptor);

    // Encode the value
    encode_varint(writer, original_value);

    // Create a reader from the same buffer
    hexi::pmc::binary_stream_reader reader(adaptor);

    // Decode the value
    T decoded_value = decode_varint<T>(reader);

    // Compare and return result
    return original_value == decoded_value;
}
```

This implementation:

1. **`encode_varint`**: Encodes an integer using standard varint encoding where each byte stores 7 bits of data and uses the MSB as a continuation flag.

2. **`decode_varint`**: Decodes a varint by reading bytes until the continuation bit is not set, accumulating the 7-bit chunks.

3. **`encode_decode_roundtrip`**: Creates a buffer with a writer, encodes the value, then creates a reader to decode it back and verifies the roundtrip is correct.

The implementation follows the varint encoding standard commonly used in Protocol Buffers and other serialization formats, which is what the Hexi library appears to support based on the test fragments.

