Looking at the code fragments, I can see that the `hexi` library has a `generate_filled` function in `hexi/shared.h` that creates a compile-time filled array. I need to use this functionality to implement the two requested functions.

Here's the complete implementation:

```cpp
#include <hexi/shared.h>
#include <array>
#include <cstdint>
#include <algorithm>

using namespace hexi::detail;

template<std::size_t Size>
constexpr auto create_filled_array(std::uint8_t value) {
    return generate_filled<Size>(value);
}

bool verify_filled_array(const auto& arr, std::uint8_t expected_value) {
    return std::ranges::all_of(arr, [expected_value](const auto& element) {
        return element == expected_value;
    });
}
```

**Explanation:**

1. **`create_filled_array`**: This function uses the `generate_filled<size>` template function from the `hexi::detail` namespace (which is in scope via `using namespace hexi::detail;`). Based on the code fragment, `generate_filled` creates a `std::array<std::uint8_t, size>` filled with the specified value at compile time using `std::ranges::fill`.

2. **`verify_filled_array`**: This function uses `std::ranges::all_of` to check if all elements in the array equal the expected value. The lambda captures `expected_value` and compares each element against it. The function returns `true` if all elements match, `false` otherwise.

Both functions are constexpr-capable where applicable, with `create_filled_array` generating the array at compile time and `verify_filled_array` being able to verify arrays at runtime (or compile time if called in a constexpr context).

