Looking at the code fragments, I can see that `hexi::buffer_underrun` is an exception class with members `read_size`, `total_read`, and `buff_size`. The exception is thrown when attempting to read more bytes than available in the buffer.

Here's the complete implementation:

```cpp
#include <hexi/exception.h>
#include <hexi/pmc/buffer_adaptor.h>
#include <vector>
#include <cstdint>

struct UnderrunInfo {
    std::size_t read_size;
    std::size_t total_read;
    std::size_t buff_size;
    bool occurred;
};

UnderrunInfo catch_buffer_underrun() {
    UnderrunInfo info{0, 0, 0, false};

    try {
        // Create a small buffer and try to read more than available
        std::vector<std::byte> buffer(5);
        hexi::pmc::buffer_adaptor<std::vector<std::byte>> adaptor(buffer);

        // Try to read more bytes than available to trigger buffer_underrun
        std::byte temp[10];
        adaptor.read(temp, 10);

    } catch (const hexi::buffer_underrun& e) {
        // Extract exception information
        info.read_size = e.read_size;
        info.total_read = e.total_read;
        info.buff_size = e.buff_size;
        info.occurred = true;
    }

    return info;
}

UnderrunInfo safe_read_with_underrun_check(hexi::pmc::buffer_adaptor<std::vector<std::byte>>& buffer, void* destination, std::size_t length) {
    UnderrunInfo info{0, 0, 0, false};

    try {
        // Attempt to read from the buffer
        buffer.read(destination, length);

    } catch (const hexi::buffer_underrun& e) {
        // Catch buffer_underrun and extract information
        info.read_size = e.read_size;
        info.total_read = e.total_read;
        info.buff_size = e.buff_size;
        info.occurred = true;
    }

    return info;
}
```

The implementation:

1. **`catch_buffer_underrun`**: Creates a scenario that triggers a `buffer_underrun` exception by attempting to read 10 bytes from a buffer that only has 5 bytes. When the exception is caught, it extracts the `read_size`, `total_read`, and `buff_size` members from the exception object and returns them in an `UnderrunInfo` struct.

2. **`safe_read_with_underrun_check`**: Attempts to read `length` bytes from the provided buffer into `destination`. If a `buffer_underrun` exception occurs during the read operation, it catches the exception, extracts the relevant information, and returns it in an `UnderrunInfo` struct with `occurred` set to `true`. If no exception occurs, `occurred` remains `false`.

