Looking at the code fragments provided, I can see how the `hexi::block_allocator` is used. Based on the test examples for `tls_block_allocator` (which wraps `block_allocator`), I can see that the allocator has an `allocate()` method that returns a pointer to an allocated object.

Here's the completed implementation:

```cpp
#include <hexi/allocators/block_allocator.h>
#include <vector>
#include <memory>

struct PoolObject {
    int id;
    double value;

    // Default constructor required by block_allocator
    PoolObject() : id(0), value(0.0) {}

    PoolObject(int i, double v) : id(i), value(v) {}
};

std::vector<PoolObject*> create_object_pool(hexi::block_allocator<PoolObject, 10>& allocator) {
    // Allocate 3 objects using the provided allocator
    std::vector<PoolObject*> pool;

    // Allocate first object
    PoolObject* obj1 = allocator.allocate();
    obj1->id = 1;
    obj1->value = 10.5;
    pool.push_back(obj1);

    // Allocate second object
    PoolObject* obj2 = allocator.allocate();
    obj2->id = 2;
    obj2->value = 20.7;
    pool.push_back(obj2);

    // Allocate third object
    PoolObject* obj3 = allocator.allocate();
    obj3->id = 3;
    obj3->value = 30.9;
    pool.push_back(obj3);

    return pool;
}
```

The implementation:
1. Creates a vector to hold pointers to the allocated objects
2. Uses `allocator.allocate()` to allocate three `PoolObject` instances from the block allocator
3. Assigns different values to each object's `id` and `value` members
4. Adds each pointer to the vector
5. Returns the vector containing all three object pointers

