Here's the complete implementation:

```cpp
#include <hexi/pmc/buffer_read_adaptor.h>
#include <hexi/pmc/buffer_write_adaptor.h>
#include <vector>
#include <cstdint>

struct TypedData {
    uint32_t id;
    int16_t value;
    double ratio;
};

std::vector<uint8_t> write_typed_data(const TypedData& data) {
    // TODO: write id, value, and ratio
    // Use write(auto& source) for each field
    std::vector<uint8_t> buffer;
    hexi::pmc::buffer_write_adaptor<std::vector<uint8_t>> writer(buffer);

    // Write each field using the template write method
    writer.write(data.id);
    writer.write(data.value);
    writer.write(data.ratio);

    return buffer;
}

TypedData read_typed_data(const std::vector<uint8_t>& buffer) {
    // TODO: read id, value, and ratio
    // Use read(T* destination) for each field
    hexi::pmc::buffer_read_adaptor<std::vector<uint8_t>> reader(buffer);

    TypedData data;

    // Read each field using the template read method with pointers
    reader.read(&data.id);
    reader.read(&data.value);
    reader.read(&data.ratio);

    return data;
}
```

The implementation:

1. **`write_typed_data`**: 
   - Creates a `std::vector<uint8_t>` buffer
   - Wraps it with `hexi::pmc::buffer_write_adaptor`
   - Uses the templated `write(auto& source)` method to write each field (uint32_t, int16_t, double)
   - Returns the populated buffer

2. **`read_typed_data`**:
   - Wraps the input buffer with `hexi::pmc::buffer_read_adaptor`
   - Uses the templated `read(T* destination)` method with pointers to each field
   - Reads values in the same order they were written (id, value, ratio)
   - Returns the populated TypedData struct

