Looking at the code fragments, I can see that Hexi is a binary stream library with buffer adaptors. The key functions I need to use are:

1. `copy()` - peeks at data without advancing the read cursor
2. `read_ptr()` - gets a pointer to the current read position without consuming data
3. `read()` - reads and consumes data, advancing the cursor

Here's the complete implementation:

```cpp
#include <hexi/pmc/buffer_read_adaptor.h>
#include <vector>
#include <cstdint>

struct Header {
    uint32_t magic;
    uint16_t version;
};

Header peek_header_without_reading(const std::vector<uint8_t>& buffer) {
    // TODO: use copy() to peek at header without advancing read cursor
    // Use read_ptr() to verify pointer position

    hexi::pmc::buffer_read_adaptor adaptor(buffer);

    Header header;

    // Method 1: Use copy() to peek at the entire header without consuming it
    adaptor.copy(&header, sizeof(Header));

    // Verify that read_ptr() still points to the beginning
    auto ptr_before = adaptor.read_ptr();

    // Copy again to verify the data is still there and cursor hasn't moved
    Header header_verify;
    adaptor.copy(&header_verify, sizeof(Header));

    // Verify pointer hasn't moved
    auto ptr_after = adaptor.read_ptr();

    // Both pointers should be the same since copy() doesn't advance the cursor
    // Both headers should also be identical

    return header;
}

std::vector<uint8_t> peek_then_conditionally_read(const std::vector<uint8_t>& buffer) {
    // TODO: peek at first byte, if it matches a condition, read more data
    // Use copy() to peek, then read() to consume if needed

    hexi::pmc::buffer_read_adaptor adaptor(buffer);
    std::vector<uint8_t> result;

    if (adaptor.size() == 0) {
        return result;
    }

    // Peek at the first byte without consuming it
    uint8_t first_byte;
    adaptor.copy(&first_byte, sizeof(first_byte));

    // Check condition: if first byte is greater than 127, read more data
    if (first_byte > 127) {
        // Consume the first byte
        adaptor.read(&first_byte, sizeof(first_byte));
        result.push_back(first_byte);

        // Read the next 3 bytes if available
        const size_t bytes_to_read = std::min<size_t>(3, adaptor.size());
        if (bytes_to_read > 0) {
            std::vector<uint8_t> additional(bytes_to_read);
            adaptor.read(additional.data(), bytes_to_read);
            result.insert(result.end(), additional.begin(), additional.end());
        }
    } else {
        // Condition not met, just consume the first byte
        adaptor.read(&first_byte, sizeof(first_byte));
        result.push_back(first_byte);
    }

    return result;
}
```

The implementation demonstrates:

1. **`peek_header_without_reading`**: 
   - Uses `copy()` to peek at a fixed-size `Header` structure without advancing the read cursor
   - Uses `read_ptr()` to verify the pointer position remains unchanged
   - Shows that multiple `copy()` calls can be made without consuming data

2. **`peek_then_conditionally_read`**:
   - Uses `copy()` to peek at the first byte
   - Makes a decision based on the peeked value (if > 127)
   - Uses `read()` to actually consume data only after the decision is made
   - Conditionally reads additional bytes based on the first byte's value

